## Acceptance

To get any licence under these terms, you must agree to them as both strict
obligations and conditions to all your licences.

## Copyright licence

The licensor grants you a copyright licence to do everything with the software
that would otherwise infringe the licensor's copyright in it, for any permitted
purpose, other than distributing the software or making changes or new works
based on the software.

## Any purpose is a permitted purpose

Commercial use, personal use, research, teaching and evaluation are all permitted
purposes. Nothing in these terms limits what you may use the software *for*. The
limits are on copying it and on changing it.

## Fetching it and running it

The licensor publishes these files at stable hash-pinned URLs so a Telegraph
validator node, or anyone else, can fetch them and run them. Fetching a copy and
running it is a permitted use for any purpose, including running it as a scoring
module on a public network and including running it inside a paid service.

## Reading it

Reading, decompiling, disassembling, instrumenting, measuring, benchmarking and
testing the software is a permitted use. Publishing what you learn by doing so is
permitted. Facts about how the software behaves are yours.

## Keeping a copy to verify

You may download and keep copies for as long as you need them to verify a
published hash, reproduce a measurement or archive what you evaluated.

## What is not granted

These terms grant no permission to:

1. distribute the software, in whole or in part, with or without a fee, whether by
   hosting it, mirroring it, committing it to a repository or serving it from a
   URL;

2. make changes or new works based on the software. This includes, and is not
   limited to, a copy with a function added, removed, renamed or re-exported; a
   copy with a constant, threshold, weight or table changed; a copy whose output is
   post-processed by code appended to it; and a copy assembled from bytes taken out
   of it;

3. present the software, or anything based on it, as your own work or under an
   identity other than the licensor's, in any registry, protocol, competition or
   marketplace.

Point 2 follows from the copyright licence above. It is spelled out because
appending one function to a compiled module and re-serving the whole file is the
specific act these terms withhold.

## Forking on GitHub

This repository is public on GitHub, and GitHub's terms of service grant every GitHub
user a licence to view and fork public repositories through GitHub's own
functionality. That grant sits above this licence and these terms do not try to
override it. Forking the repository on GitHub is fine.

Note what that grant covers, in GitHub's own words: to "use, display, perform and
reproduce (by forking)". It does not grant the right to make changes or new works, and
GitHub says broader permissions have to come from the repository's own licence. This
licence does not give them. So a fork is a copy you may keep. It is not permission to
change a binary and publish the changed version, whether from a fork's raw URL or
anywhere else.

## Fair use

You may have fair use or fair dealing rights under the law. These terms do not
limit them.

## No other rights

These terms do not allow you to sublicense or transfer any of your licences to
anyone else, and do not prevent the licensor from granting licences to anyone
else. They imply no other licences.

## Patent licence

The licensor grants you a patent licence for the software that covers patent
claims the licensor can license, or becomes able to license, that you would
infringe by using the software.

## Patent defence

If you make any written claim that the software infringes or contributes to
infringement of any patent, your patent licence for the software ends
immediately. If your company makes such a claim, your patent licence ends
immediately for work on behalf of your company.

## Violations

The first time you are notified in writing that you have violated these terms, or
done something with the software not covered by your licences, your licences can
continue if you come into full compliance and take practical steps to correct past
violations within 32 days of receiving that notice. Otherwise all your licences end
immediately.

## No liability

***As far as the law allows, the software comes as is, without any warranty or
condition, and the licensor will not be liable to you for any damages arising out
of these terms or the use or nature of the software, under any kind of legal
claim.***

## Definitions

The **licensor** is zkasuran. The **software** is the files in this repository
that the licensor authored and makes available under these terms. Files authored by
others are listed in `NOTICE` and are not covered here: they keep the licence their
own author gave them.

**You** refers to the individual or entity agreeing to these terms.

**Your company** is any legal entity, sole proprietorship or other organisation
you work for, plus every organisation that controls it, that it controls, or that
is under common control with it. **Control** means ownership of substantially all
the assets of an entity, or the power to direct its management and policies by
vote, contract or otherwise. Control can be direct or indirect.

**Your licences** are all the licences granted to you for the software under these
terms.

**Use** means anything you do with the software requiring one of your licences.

## Earlier releases stay MIT

Files published in this repository at commits up to and including
`9250395a8131e4f7da51ab548d455c7270d4acd3` were released under the MIT licence.
That grant is not withdrawn and could not be: a copy obtained under MIT stays
under MIT for whoever obtained it. `LICENSE-HISTORY.md` states the exact scope and
how to tell which files it covers.

## Drafting note

The structure and much of the plain-language wording of this licence is adapted
from a standardised source-available licence template, with its noncommercial
restriction replaced by the "any purpose" grant above and its identifying
references removed, as that template's own terms require of an adaptation.
